---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TxgPipeline from "../../components/TxgPipeline.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D24.1): the question, a plain
// answer, the figure, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in
// the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("txg");
const T = "module/zfs/txg.c";
const L = {
  quiesceAll: gh(T, 398, 399),
  size: gh("include/sys/txg.h", 42, 45),
  perCpu: gh("include/sys/txg_impl.h", 51, 55),
  assign: gh("module/zfs/dmu_tx.c", 1085),
  rele: gh(T, 372, 375),
  startSync: gh(T, 601),
  timeout: gh(T, 116),
  scanActive: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 3723, 3732),
  waitSynced: gh(T, 714, 717),
  ioctl: gh("module/zfs/zfs_ioctl.c", 7306),
  kick: gh(T, 830, 832),
  dirtySpace: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 997, 1002),
  syncPct: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 120),
  dirtyMax: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 99, 105),
  unlock: gh(T, 412, 413),
  holdOpen: gh(T, 333, 334),
  askQuiesce: gh(T, 574, 576),
  handoff: gh(T, 662, 666),
  waitOpen: gh(T, 791, 794),
  longFree: gh("module/zfs/dmu.c", 1096, 1106),
  passes: gh(T, 86, 90),
  converge: gh(T, 92, 99),
  rewrite: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 128),
  ubChanged: gh("module/zfs/uberblock.c", 74),
  flush: gh("module/zfs/vdev_label.c", 2085, 2098),
  labels: gh("module/zfs/vdev_label.c", 2109, 2154),
  rewriteConfig: gh("module/zfs/spa.c", 10958),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="txg"
  title="When does my write reach the disk? Transaction groups · Uberblok"
  description="ZFS gathers every change into a numbered transaction group, writes the group out every few seconds and commits it with one uberblock write. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>When does my write reach the disk?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          Usually a few seconds after the program that made it has moved on. ZFS never changes a block in place: each
          change is written to a new block, the block that points to it is rewritten too, and so on up to the uberblock,
          the root record from which ZFS finds everything else in the pool. Doing all that for every single write, with
          a flush of each disk's cache every time, would be very slow.
        </p>
        <p>
          So ZFS gathers changes in memory into a numbered batch called a transaction group, or txg. Every change joins
          the txg that is open at that moment. Every five seconds, or sooner when a lot of changed data is waiting or a
          sync is asked for, ZFS closes the open txg and opens the next one at once, so programs keep writing. It waits
          for the changes already under way in the closed txg to finish, writes the whole txg to the disks, and ends
          with a single write of a new uberblock. That write is the commit. Before it, the pool starts from the previous
          txg. After it, the pool starts from the new one.
        </p>
        <p>
          After a power cut, the pool comes back at the last committed txg, whole. The changes of the txgs that were
          not committed yet are lost, usually a few seconds' worth. A program that cannot lose them, such as a
          database, asks for its data to be on disk with fsync. ZFS then writes the change to its intent log, the ZIL,
          without waiting for the txg.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="pipeline">
      <h2 id="pipeline"><span class="n">1</span> Three txgs, one per stage</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each card is a txg. Two writes are under way in txg 41, drawn as dots on the CPUs they run on. Press Next
          event to move one step at a time. The outlined part of the figure is what that step changed. Left alone, the
          writes end, and five seconds after the last sync began the sync thread asks for the open txg. Txg 41 moves to
          the middle, and txg 42 opens behind it to take the new writes. Then txg 41 moves right and is written in
          passes, and the line On disk changes when its uberblock is written.
        </p>
        <p>
          Press Reset, then <code>zpool sync</code> at once. The sync thread does not wait for the five seconds, and txg
          41 stays in the middle until its two writes are done. Writes you add join whichever txg is open. Free a large
          file while a txg is being written: ZFS asks for a new txg at once, so that one txg does not fill up with
          frees, and all three stages are busy. That is the most there can be. Play runs it all in slow time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <TxgPipeline lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Two kernel threads per pool move txgs along, in <code>txg.c</code>. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken
        from release zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Joining the open txg</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A write first joins a txg with <code>txg_hold_open</code>. It reads the number of the open txg and raises a
          count of writes in that txg. There is one count per CPU, so writers on different CPUs never wait for each
          other. When the write is done, the count goes down again.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>txg_hold_open</code>, txg.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={T}
            from={333}
            to={338}
            symbol="txg_hold_open"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 333,
                to: 334,
                text: `The lock of this CPU, then the open txg's number. <code>dmu_tx_assign</code> <a href="${L.assign}">calls this</a> for every transaction. To close a txg, the quiesce thread <a href="${L.quiesceAll}">takes the lock of every CPU</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 337,
                text: `This CPU's count for the txg, in <a href="${L.size}">an array of 4</a> indexed by the txg's low two bits. One count per CPU, because a single one <a href="${L.perCpu}">“would result in heavy lock contention”</a>. <code>txg_rele_to_sync</code> <a href="${L.rele}">lowers it</a> when the transaction is done.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>When the sync thread wakes up</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          The sync thread sleeps in a loop. It wakes when five seconds have passed since the last sync began, when
          someone waits for a sync, or when a closed txg is ready for it. A scrub or resilver in progress keeps it from
          sleeping at all.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>txg_sync_thread</code>, txg.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={T}
            from={542}
            to={553}
            symbol="txg_sync_thread"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 542,
                text: `<code>timer</code> counts from <code>start</code>, set when <a href="${L.startSync}">the last <code>spa_sync</code> began</a>. The timeout is <code>zfs_txg_timeout</code>, <a href="${L.timeout}">5 seconds</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 543,
                to: 546,
                text: `It sleeps while all of these hold: no scan is active (<a href="${L.scanActive}">a scrub, a resilver, or a background free</a>), the timer has not run out, nobody waits for a txg past the last synced one, and no quiesced txg is waiting.`,
              },
              {
                from: 545,
                text: `<code>zpool sync</code> <a href="${L.ioctl}">calls <code>txg_wait_synced(dp, 0)</code></a>, which <a href="${L.waitSynced}">raises <code>tx_sync_txg_waiting</code></a> to the open txg plus 2. So does <a href="${L.kick}"><code>txg_kick</code></a>, which <a href="${L.dirtySpace}"><code>dsl_pool_dirty_space</code> calls</a> once a txg's dirty data passes <a href="${L.syncPct}">20%</a> of <a href="${L.dirtyMax}"><code>zfs_dirty_data_max</code></a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Closing a txg without stopping the writers</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          The quiesce thread closes the open txg in <code>txg_quiesce</code>. It holds every CPU's lock for an instant,
          long enough to raise the open txg's number by one. From then on, new writes join the next txg.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>txg_quiesce</code>, the switch, txg.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={T}
            from={398}
            to={402}
            symbol="txg_quiesce"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 398,
                to: 399,
                text: `Every CPU's <code>tc_open_lock</code>, the lock <a href="${L.holdOpen}"><code>txg_hold_open</code> takes</a> before it reads the open txg. No writer can be halfway into the old txg.`,
              },
              {
                from: 402,
                text: `The next txg is open. The locks are <a href="${L.unlock}">released right after</a>, so writers do not wait for the old txg to drain.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Waiting for the writes under way</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Writes that joined the old txg before the switch still have to finish. The quiesce thread waits until every
          CPU's count for that txg is back to zero. Then nothing can change the txg any more, and it is handed over to
          the sync thread.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>txg_quiesce</code>, the drain, txg.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={T}
            from={422}
            to={428}
            symbol="txg_quiesce"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 425,
                to: 426,
                text: `It sleeps while this CPU still holds the txg. <code>txg_rele_to_sync</code> <a href="${L.rele}">wakes it</a> when the count reaches 0.`,
              },
              {
                from: 428,
                text: `After the last CPU, the txg is quiesced. The quiesce thread <a href="${L.handoff}">hands it to the sync thread</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>One txg per stage</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          The quiesce thread closes a txg only when someone asks, and only once the txg it closed last has been taken by
          the sync thread. That is why there is never more than one txg quiescing. Usually the sync thread asks when it
          is ready for more work, so the next txg stays open while one is written.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>txg_quiesce_thread</code>, txg.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={T}
            from={638}
            to={641}
            symbol="txg_quiesce_thread"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 639,
                text: `Nobody has asked for a txg past the open one. The sync thread <a href="${L.askQuiesce}">asks when it needs a txg</a>. The other caller is <a href="${L.waitOpen}"><code>txg_wait_open</code></a>, which at this release <a href="${L.longFree}"><code>dmu_free_long_range</code> calls</a> “to avoid filling up a TXG with just frees”.`,
              },
              {
                from: 640,
                text: "The txg quiesced last is still waiting for the sync thread.",
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Writing until nothing changes</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>spa_sync</code> writes the txg in passes. The first writes the changed data and the metadata above it.
          Writing takes space, and the space maps that record it are themselves metadata, so the next pass writes them,
          and so on. The loop ends when a pass leaves nothing new to write.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>spa_sync_iterate_to_convergence</code>, spa.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/spa.c"
            from={10749}
            to={10765}
            symbol="spa_sync_iterate_to_convergence"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 10749,
                to: 10761,
                text: "After pass 1, if the root block pointer was not born in this txg and the MOS, the pool's own metadata, is clean, nothing changed: the txg is a no-op.",
              },
              {
                from: 10765,
                text: `Another pass while the MOS is dirty in this txg. <a href="${L.passes}">Only the first pass writes user data</a>. The later ones <a href="${L.converge}">are kept small so the loop ends</a>: from <a href="${L.rewrite}">pass 2</a>, a block written earlier in this txg is overwritten where it is.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The commit, or nothing at all</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Once the passes are done, <code>vdev_config_sync</code> flushes the disks and writes the new uberblock. The
          room on <a href={roomUrl(lang, "tree")}>the tree and the uberblock</a> follows that write. A txg in which
          nothing changed stops here, before the flush: an idle pool goes through a txg every five seconds without
          writing to its disks.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_config_sync</code>, vdev_label.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/vdev_label.c"
            from={2071}
            to={2077}
            symbol="vdev_config_sync"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 2072,
                to: 2073,
                text: `The new uberblock takes this txg's number. <code>changed</code> is true <a href="${L.ubChanged}">if the root block pointer was born in this txg</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 2075,
                to: 2077,
                text: `Nothing changed, no vdev configuration is dirty, multihost protection is off: return before <a href="${L.flush}">the flush</a> and <a href="${L.labels}">the label and uberblock writes</a>. <code>spa_sync</code> <a href="${L.rewriteConfig}">calls this</a> after the passes.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the two threads' wait conditions as the code writes them, over the same variables. It draws 4
        CPUs. Each write is one block, and the threshold that starts a sync early is scaled to 4 blocks (20% of a
        20-block <code>zfs_dirty_data_max</code>). Time is slowed, and the length of a write or a pass is made up. It
        leaves out the write throttle (writers delayed as dirty data grows), the short wait of a writer while a txg
        closes, scrubs and resilvers, administrative tasks that run inside a sync, the ZIL, and pool suspension. Pass
        sizes are illustrative: a txg with data always takes three passes here.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="txg" />
</Base>

